WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.302
Here is how the wavelet version produces a forecast.

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:14.236
With the Haar filter at level one, each MODWT detail is half the difference of consecutive returns. w t equals r t minus r t minus one, over two.

00:00:14.686 --> 00:00:20.438
Written with the backshift operator B, that is one minus B over two, applied to the returns.

00:00:20.888 --> 00:00:23.901
That factor adds one extra moving average lag.

00:00:24.351 --> 00:00:29.966
So if returns follow an ARMA p q, the detail series naturally needs about q plus one.

00:00:30.416 --> 00:00:36.139
Our pipeline fits ARMA plus GARCH to the detail series and forecasts its mean and variance.

00:00:36.589 --> 00:00:38.369
Then we undo the transform.

00:00:38.819 --> 00:00:43.109
Tomorrow's return equals today's return plus two times tomorrow's detail.

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:46.424
So the return mean is r t plus two mu w.

00:00:46.874 --> 00:00:51.257
And because we multiplied by two, the variance is multiplied by four.

00:00:51.707 --> 00:01:00.470
Finally, both the mean and the variance feed the same target forecast: variance, plus the squared gap between the predicted mean and the twenty-day average.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:06.242
Raw, DWT and MODWT all end with this same formula.
